        <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Overview</b></h6>
        <p class="text-justify">The Belau National Hospital (BNH) is Palau?s only hospital and the top of the in-country health system. It is publicly owned, opened in 1994, and is located in Meyuns, Koror. It has 80 beds. MHHS currently operates it through its hospital bureau, which the Ministry?s website calls the Bureau of Hospital Services and the national government website calls the Bureau of Hospital &amp; Clinical Services. BNH receives cases from every Community Health Center. It is also the only point from which patients are referred abroad for care that cannot be provided in Palau.</p>
        <p class="text-justify"><strong>Note:</strong> The legal operator of BNH is in transition. The Belau National Hospital Authority Act (RPPL 11-16, signed 26 January 2022; 34 PNC Sections 351?356) creates a Belau National Hospital Authority. The Authority is ?an independent organization located within the Executive Branch, separate from the Ministry of Health and Human Services,? and its Board is responsible for all aspects of operating the hospital. However, operational authority passes to the Authority only after five statutory preconditions are met (34 PNC Section 356): a transition plan affirmed by a Joint Resolution of the Olbiil Era Kelulau, legislative amendments, an enacted budget, a study on keeping grant funding, and a report on Compact funding. The Minister must then issue a Determination of Completion. The Board was fully constituted in December 2024, but as of late 2025 the transition plan was not complete and the FY2026 budget kept hospital management under MHHS. S.B. No. 12-77, which would give the Authority administrative independence, passed first reading on 20 August 2026 and is still pending. Classify BNH as ?National Hospital ? public (MHHS-operated),? and re-check the operator before relying on it. Once the transfer happens, the Authority will be exempt from all licensing requirements (34 PNC Section 355(c)).</p>

        <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Role</b></h6>
        <ul>
          <li>Serve as the only inpatient hospital in Palau and the national referral point for all Community Health Centers</li>
          <li>Provide 24-hour emergency care through the Emergency Room, plus outpatient care through the Outpatient Department (OPD). The Bureau of Public Health lists the OPD among the primary-care clinics it operates.</li>
          <li>Act as the gateway for off-island referral. A patient may be referred abroad only if the Medical Referral Committee approves; the Committee has five voting physicians and approval needs 75% of their votes. Referral is not allowed for a condition that can be treated adequately in Palau (34 PNC Section 333). Partner referral sites are in Taiwan, the Philippines, India and Hawaii.</li>
          <li>Host visiting-specialist clinics in specialties not available locally, including pediatric cardiology, neurology, dermatology, ENT, ophthalmology, urology, orthopedics and nephrology.</li>
        </ul>

        <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Clinical Services</b></h6>
        <p><strong>Core Capability Areas</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Inpatient and outpatient general medical care; Emergency Room; Operating Room services</li>
          <li>Maternity / obstetric ward, hemodialysis unit, hyperbaric medicine unit, and telemedicine services</li>
          <li>Clinical dentistry at the BNH Dental Clinic Unit, run by the Bureau of Public Health?s Division of Oral Health</li>
          <li>By law, the hospital?s development goal is to reach ?full secondary and early tertiary level care coverage on island? (34 PNC Section 355(e)). This means current capability is below full secondary level, and complex or specialist cases are routinely referred abroad.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Diagnostic &amp; Support Infrastructure</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Laboratory, pharmacy, rehabilitation and recovery services, run by the Division of Ancillary Services</li>
          <li>Clinical epidemiology, medical supply, food service, and facilities and equipment departments</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>80 beds, in a single facility. An 80-bed replacement hospital near the airport is being planned, with an estimated cost of $398.8 million; its feasibility study is due in March 2027.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-justify"><em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: 34 PNC Sections 101, 331?333 and 351?356. Bed count, opening year and replacement plan come from Pacific Island Times (3 September 2026) and Island Times (August 2025). Department and unit detail comes from the MHHS website (healthypalau.pw). None of these details are stated in the Code itself.</em></p>
